A||N||14||grinthock||Fiero Electronics Question - Sensor Readings||||5||
Z||000000||grinthock||05-14-2001||08:52 PM||||I am looking to THIS Forum for the answers to these questions, I am trying to make some sense of these sensor readings... here are my questions.... Thanks in advance for the help<P>1)  Map sensor, what voltage == what boost/vac level  (eg 1.5v = -5lbs)<P>2)  O2 Sensor, what voltage == what PPM of O2 in the exhaust<P>3) What are the integrator, Block Lean Multiplier (BLM) and O2 crosscount values for?  What do they mean?<P>4) What is base pulse width (assuming something to do with spark)<P>5) Are there changes I can make to improve these values to something more optimum?<P><BR>Thanks everyone!||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||05-14-2001||09:08 PM||||Well, the only info that I can find says that the 02 sensor has variable voltage from .1 volts to 1 volt.  The ECM sees anything below .35 volts as lean and above .55 volts as rich.<P>The MAP sensor varies between 1 and 2 volts, depending on vacuum and barometric pressure.<P>I assume that you are trying to fool the ecm somehow.  You might be able to creep a few HP out of it, but your fuel mileage will suffer.  All those type of sensors are there to control the emissions of cars and to increase fuel economy.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000002||grinthock||05-14-2001||09:11 PM||||But I am looking for specific values, what V = what value.....   It's not to trick the ECM, but diagnose issues||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||05-14-2001||09:24 PM||||the block learn values.  one is short term, last few key (run) cycles. other is long term ie. since ecm has had battery voltage. Base value it 128 counts.  so if you scan it and see long term is 132 your engine constantly has required more fuel to keep the correct air/fuel mixture at 14.7 to 1.   So if your short term is more than your long term it means that lately your engine has required more fuel to keep 14.7 to 1 balance. Also if you scan your short term and than take your car out and drive it differently than you normally do-ie foot to the floor accell, you will see it "relearning" to suit driving style.  Hope this helps.  But block learn has not been all-that helpful for diag.||||reg||1
Z||000004||grinthock||05-14-2001||09:32 PM||||but that's the kinda stuff I wanna know!  Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||05-15-2001||01:55 AM||||1) Map sensor, what voltage == what boost/vac level (eg 1.5v = -5lbs)<P>0-5V = 0-100kPa for a 1BAR map.<BR>0-5V = 0-200kPa for a 2BAR map.<BR>0-5V = 0-300kPa for a 3Bar map.<P>You can convert to PSI (Atmosphere). <P>2) O2 Sensor, what voltage == what PPM of O2 in the exhaust<P>* Can't be done. It's a bang-bang controller. The O2 is only correct at .456V = 14.73:1 below that it's lean but the AFR is not linear. Same as above. Rich but not linear. You need a wideband to be able to crossreference an O2 voltage to AFR. If you need a reference point, we generally consider below 800mV on the O2 at WOT to be getting too lean for comfort. An EGT is needed to be more accurate.<P>3) What are the integrator, Block Lea(R)n Multiplier (BLM) and O2 crosscount values for? What do they mean?<P>BLM is the block learn multiplier. Long term correction. It is a scaling factor that the ECM uses to get an even crossing of the O2 stoich value (14.7:1) as compared to the Volumetric Efficency tables in the ECM. <P>Assume the VE tables are dead on, the BLM will be 128. If higher, it's adding fuel, if lower it's removing fuel. Unless it hits the limits (I have to look for sure, but 108 on the low end and 148 on the high end I believe, the ECM is still able to control the fuel). Beyond that it can't and the engine will go rich or lean.<P>INTegrator is a short term correction meaning essentially the same thing, but for very short duration changes (changes in mere seconds based on the TPS and MAP). The INT will move in response to a fueling need change (short term) and the BLM will move in responses to a long term change.<P>The BLM cell is a 4X4 matrix of RPM vs Kpa manifold pressure. The ECM will learn BLM values to stuff in there to allow the BLM to be as close to 128 as possible. One cell is dedicated to idle (usually 16). <P>O2 cross-counts are the number of O2 lean-rich-lean transitions. The ECM's objective is to drive the fueling (via the injector pulse widths) to get the transitions to be even across .456V assuming you aren't in WOT.<P>That's the Cliff Notes version, but basically it.<P>4) What is base pulse width (assuming something to do with spark)<P>Base Pulse Width is the starting pulse width for the injectors to deliver fuel. From there, other factors are used to add or subtract fuel. <BR> <BR>WOT adds another level of detail.<P>5) Are there changes I can make to improve these values to something more optimum?<P>Why wouldn't GM have made them optimum in the first place? GM does a dang good job and other than some minor tweaks for your specific engine (considering they based the calibrations on the statistical results of 15 engines) or if you made big changes to the engine, there is nothing to gain.<P>And therein lies the Vietnam War.....<P>Ok, for me, the keys to the city are the BLM and O2. The BLM tells me if the engine is pulling or adding fuel. Only usable at cruise (ie. not WOT). The O2 is good for checking both cruise and WOT. If I'm hitting 950mV+, that's just too much fuel. I will back it down to 800-850mV. On SC engines, I stay above 850 and watch for knock counts. Knock bad ju-ju.<P>The trick to reading the BLM is a steady foot. Cruise the car as different RPM and load by stay out of WOT. The Fiero goes into WOT at something like 12% TPS (very low). The ECM will move the BLM to keep the INT at 128. If the BLM is more than 10 counts in either direction, I assume something is up. If you've made engine changes, then the BLM will tell you if the ECM has any hope of controlling the fuel in closed loop. Any change in the throttle will cause a changing in the map and thus a change in the INT, so a steady foot is a must. Then floor it and watch the O2. On a stock calibration, the O2 will be between 850mV and 1000mV. Not unusual to be that high. GM runs 'em rich.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-15-2001).]||||reg||1
